      "latin": "discus, discī m.",
      "status": "recommended",
      "period": "modern",
      "gloss": "a disk (of a computer); a disc",
      "evidence": [
        "Traupman 260, 305",
        "M-O s.v. CD"
      ],
      "comment": "Traupman’s word for a computer disk; Morgan–Owens gives <span lang=\"la\">discus mūsicus</span> for a music CD. Classically <span lang=\"la\">discus</span> is a quoit, thrown in the games (Hor. C. 1.8.11), then a dish shaped like one (Apul.) and the disc of a sundial (Vitr. 9.9.1); its modern sense keeps the shape."
    },
    {
      "latin": "dūra statiō",
      "status": "alternative",
      "period": "modern",
      "gloss": "a hard drive",
      "evidence": [
        "Traupman 261, 326"
      ],
      "comment": "Traupman’s phrase, a calque of the English. Where a disk is meant, <span lang=\"la\">discus</span> serves as well."
    },
    {
      "latin": "compāctus discus",
      "status": "alternative",
      "period": "modern",
      "gloss": "a CD, compact disc",
      "evidence": [
        "Traupman 260, 294",
